            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To discuss interprofessional collaboration between the primary oncology clinicians and specialists in the management of immune-related ocular toxicities.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA SOURCES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Peer-reviewed articles, case reports and systematic reviews.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Accurate ophthalmologic assessment is critical for the prevention and treatment of ocular toxicities associated with immunotherapy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="IMPLICATIONS FOR NURSING PRACTICE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Oncology nurses play a key role in early identification and management of ocular symptoms from immunotherapy; early referral to ophthalmic specialists can enhance recovery and preserve sight.</AbstractText>
